    "result_text": "No other code paths emit those statuses \u2014 every 400/409/429 flows through an escalation handler. The change is complete.\n\n## Summary\n\nThe specification requires that all rejection responses (400, 409, 429) return an RFC7807 `application/problem+json` body with `type`, `title`, `status`, and `detail`.\n\nOn inspection, nearly every rejection handler already produced a `ProblemDetail` via the shared `ProblemDetails.build(...)` helper. The **one exception** was `MissingOwnerFieldsExceptionHandler` (the 400 for missing/blank required owner fields \u2014 the exact path the new test exercises by removing `city`), which still returned a bespoke `MissingFieldsError` JSON record instead of problem+json.\n\n**Change made** \u2014 `MissingOwnerFieldsExceptionHandler.java`:\n- Now builds its body with the same `ProblemDetails.build(ex, HttpStatus.BAD_REQUEST, ...)` helper the other handlers use, so it gets the RFC7807 `type`/`title`/`status`/`detail` members and `application/problem+json` content type for free.\n- Preserves the offending field names by attaching them as an `errors` property on the `ProblemDetail`, so no diagnostic information is lost.\n- Removed the now-unused `MissingFieldsError` record.\n\nThis keeps the logic where it belongs (the shared `ProblemDetails` builder stays the single source of the common problem+json shape; the handler only adds its endpoint-specific `errors` detail) rather than duplicating the RFC7807 assembly.\n\nTest suite: `BUILD SUCCESS`, `Tests run: 1, Failures: 0, Errors: 0` \u2014 the new `coreRejectionIsProblemJson` acceptance test passes, asserting the 400 response is `application/problem+json` with `$.status == 400`.",
